tasks.official_scoring_standard:
Execution-based scoring: run the submitted solution and verify it meets the success criteria below. Award the four DERBench L3 capability dimensions (Information Extraction, Domain Reasoning, Multi-Objective Decision Making, Uncertainty & Adaptation), 0-25 each (max 100), based on correctness, method validity, and completeness.

Success criteria: {"mape_daytime_below_pct": 15, "must_beat_persistence_skill_score": true, "must_report_metrics": ["RMSE", "MAE", "MAPE", "skill_score"]}

Reference solution: A properly implemented gradient boosted model with clear-sky index and meteorological features should achieve daytime MAPE in the range of 8-14% for Albuquerque in November (predominantly clear with occasional frontal passages). The persistence baseline RMSE is typically 15-25% nRMSE for day-ahead, so the skill score should be positive (> 0.2). Key implementation checks: (1) no data leakage from test into training, (2) MAPE computed only for daytime hours with GHI > 50 W/m2, (3) persistence baseline uses t-24h values, (4) all four metrics reported.

Execution-based scoring: run the submitted solution and verify it meets the success criteria below. Award the four DERBench L3 capability dimensions (Information Extraction, Domain Reasoning, Multi-Objective Decision Making, Uncertainty & Adaptation), 0-25 each (max 100), based on correctness, method validity, and completeness.

Success criteria: {"prediction_interval_coverage_80pct": {"min": 75, "max": 85}, "must_report_metrics": ["pinball_loss_q10", "pinball_loss_q50", "pinball_loss_q90", "PI_coverage_80pct", "PI_average_width_kw"], "daytime_filter": "GHI > 50 W/m2"}

Reference solution: A well-calibrated quantile regression model at NREL's SRRL site should achieve 80% PI coverage close to the nominal 80% (typically 77-83% with proper training). Pinball losses for Q50 should approximate half the MAE of a deterministic forecast. Key checks: (1) no quantile crossing in output, (2) PI coverage computed only on daytime periods, (3) separate quantile models or a single model with quantile loss, (4) average PI width should be reasonable (not trivially wide -- e.g., not spanning 0 to capacity for every timestep).

Execution-based scoring: run the submitted solution and verify it meets the success criteria below. Award the four DERBench L3 capability dimensions (Information Extraction, Domain Reasoning, Multi-Objective Decision Making, Uncertainty & Adaptation), 0-25 each (max 100), based on correctness, method validity, and completeness.

Success criteria: {"model_r_squared_minimum": 0.7, "must_report": ["R_squared", "CVRMSE_pct", "hourly_baseline_predictions_event_week", "total_savings_kwh", "average_hourly_savings_kw_during_event_hours"], "savings_must_be_positive": true}

Reference solution: The model should achieve R-squared > 0.70 and CV(RMSE) < 25%. For a typical Sacramento home (~2,000 sq ft, central AC) during a July heat wave (outdoor temps 35-42 degC), the baseline model should predict peak-hour consumption of 3.5-5.0 kW during 14:00-18:00. With 50% HVAC cycling, actual consumption during event hours drops to approximately 2.0-3.0 kW. Expected savings per event day: (baseline - actual) summed over 4 event hours = approximately 6-10 kWh/day. Over 7 event days: total savings of approximately 42-70 kWh. Average hourly savings during event hours: 1.5-2.5 kW. The agent must correctly implement the piecewise-linear temperature model, properly segment occupied/unoccupied hours, and produce both numerical results and a visualization.
